186518147 #edb/ref 1074 "The equation of forced harmonic motion without damping is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=f(t).\n$$\n\nThe complementary solution ${y}_{c}$ here represents simple harmonic motion, and it can be written as\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t,\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are arbitrary constants of integration.\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=18\\,⟹\\,\\omega =3\\sqrt{2}.\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\sqrt{2}t)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\sqrt{2}t).\n$$\n\nThe oscillator experiences resonance when the frequency of the external periodic force $f(t)=6{sin}^{2}⁡pt$ is equal to the frequency of the complementary solution, which is $\\omega =3\\sqrt{2}.$ Since \n\n$$\nf(t)=6{sin}^{2}⁡pt=3-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡2pt,\n$$\n\nthe frequency of the external periodic force is $2p.$\n\nTherefore, the oscillator experiences resonance when $p=\\frac{\\omega }{2}=\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518240 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186518266 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518240 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "373883cf-0b34-53c8-a1b3-d0897fad83b2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518240 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186518147 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518259 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186518240 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518259 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "6ea7ee19-1db2-5615-9f9e-e31df6e39383" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518259 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186518148 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518266 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186518317 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518266 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "88f88f3d-b294-5d78-b139-7b4adde6210f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518266 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186495094 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518302 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "d88d67e5-0c3f-5914-b3bd-6949d7868fd1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518302 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186317433 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518317 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 175921