ot ⟨x + 1,\\,y + 2⟩ & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186521684 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186521726 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186521684 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186521684 #edb/ref 1071 "Given the function $f(x,y)=- 5 x^{2} + 5 x y^{2} + 2 y^{2}$, find the equation of the tangent line to the level curve that passes through the point where $(x,y)=(- \\frac{3}{2},\\frac{5}{2}).$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186521684 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "20ccc6f9-9078-5553-ae96-b36095f293ae" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186521684 #edb/ref 1074 "The equation of the tangent line to the level curve $f(x,y)=k$ at the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n∇f({x}_{0},{y}_{0})\\cdot ⟨x-{x}_{0},\\,y-{y}_{0}⟩=0.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the gradient of $f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at $(x,y)=(- \\frac{3}{2},\\frac{5}{2}):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(- 5 x^{2} + 5 x y^{2} + 2 y^{2})\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(- 5 x^{2} + 5 x y^{2} + 2 y^{2})\\,j \\\\  & =(- 10 x + 5 y^{2})\\,i+(10 x y + 4 y)\\,j \\\\ ∇f(- \\frac{3}{2},\\frac{5}{2}) & =(- 10 (- \\frac{3}{2}) + 5 (\\frac{5}{2})^{2})\\,i+(10 (- \\frac{3}{2}) (\\frac{5}{2}) + 4 (\\frac{5}{2}))\\,j \\\\  & =(\\frac{185}{4})\\,i+(- \\frac{55}{2})\\,j \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{185}{4},- \\frac{55}{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the Cartesian equation of the tangent line to the level curve that passes through the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f({x}_{0},{y}_{0})\\cdot ⟨x-{x}_{0},\\,y-{y}_{0}⟩ & =0 \\\\ ∇f(- \\frac{3}{2},\\frac{5}{2})\\cdot ⟨x-(- \\frac{3}{2}),\\,y-(\\frac{5}{2})⟩ & =0 \\\\ ⟨\\frac{185}{4},- \\frac{55}{2}⟩\\cdot ⟨x + \\frac{3}{2},\\,y - \\frac{5}{2}⟩ & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186521695 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186521715 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186521695 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186521695 #ed